# shell: desktop
# home: fixture
# prefs: factory
# setup: stall
# A turn that does not end is written down, by something outside it.
#
# A freeze leaves no signal and no exit: nothing a crash handler does
# would catch one, and the shell cannot notice about itself that it has
# stopped noticing.  So something else counts its turns, and when the
# count stops it asks the stuck thread -- by a signal -- to say where it
# is.  An application loaded into this process holds a turn for eight
# seconds, which is the freeze, done on purpose.
wait 40
note the turn is being held now; the watch says so after five seconds
wait 500
note what was written names the trouble, and how long it had lasted
expect-file-has /tmp/trk_reports/latest-stall.txt a turn that has not ended
note and WHERE the stuck turn was, which is the whole of what a freeze
note otherwise refuses to say
expect-file-has /tmp/trk_reports/latest-stall.txt the_turn_that_will_not_end
note the shell is still here afterwards, and answering: a watch that
note killed it would lose the very thing somebody wanted to read
expect-read trail:quiet a turn quiet for 5000 ms
quit
